8.1  Who listens on which port

TL;DR
  - A program that accepts connections listens on a port: 22 for SSH, 443 for
    HTTPS.
  - On 0.0.0.0 or [::], it accepts connections from every network. On 127.0.0.1
    or [::1], only from this machine.
  - ss lists them. Seeing which process owns another user's port needs root.

      ss -tulpn          # TCP, UDP, listening, with processes, numeric
      sudo ss -tulpn

On your machine
  TCP       0.0.0.0:22      all interfaces     user root
  TCP       [::]:22         all interfaces     user root
  TCP       127.0.0.1:631   this machine only  user root
  TCP       127.0.0.1:8080  this machine only  supervisor (PID 4100)
  UDP       0.0.0.0:5353    all interfaces     user avahi
  note: the process of a socket owned by another user needs root: sudo ss -tulpn

Pitfalls
  - netstat is the old tool (package net-tools), often not installed: ss
    replaced it.
  - Listening on 0.0.0.0 doesn't mean reachable from the Internet: a firewall or
    your router can still block it.
  - Ports published by Docker bypass ufw rules, because Docker writes its own
    firewall rules.
  - Listening on a port under 1024 needs root or the CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE
    capability.
  - "Address already in use" means another process holds the port: find it with
    sudo ss -ltnp 'sport = :8080'.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 8.1 --why

Related
  Commands  ss
  Paths     /proc/net/tcp
  Hub       idk network Network
  See also  idk 3.2 setuid, setgid and sticky bit
